  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Method</p>
    <h1>How the numbers are worked out</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The number at the top of the site is never typed in. It is worked out from the seven pieces of the equation, each carrying a range drawn from what has been published, by a method that anyone can run from the public repository.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section who" id="who">
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Who is asking</p>
      <p>I'm John, and I write about AI, sentience and the spread of conscious life beyond Earth at <a href="https://sentient-horizons.com" rel="noopener">Sentient Horizons</a>. This is the sister of <a href="https://beltequation.com" rel="noopener">The Belt Equation</a>, where I track the odds of leaving Earth myself. Here the question is older and larger: whether anyone else is out there, and why we cannot tell. I wanted a place where the argument is laid out piece by piece, every number carries its source, and a new discovery moves the answer in public.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Why the name?</p>
      <p>Because that is the observed fact. The sky is quiet. Every explanation on this site is an answer to that one observation, and the site does not presume which answer is right: it works out, from the published numbers, how surprised we should be.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section narrow prose">
    <h2>The multiplication</h2>
    <p>
      In 1961 the astronomer Frank Drake wrote down a way to organise the question "how many civilisations in our galaxy could we detect right now?" as a multiplication of seven quantities: how many stars are born each year; what share of them have planets; how many planets per star could support life; on what share of those life starts; on what share of those it becomes intelligent; what share of intelligent species make themselves detectable; and how long they stay detectable. Multiply the seven and you have N, the number of detectable civilisations. The equation is a way of dividing one unanswerable question into seven smaller ones, some of which can now be answered.
    </p>

    <h2>Why a spread, not a point</h2>
    <p>
      For decades people plugged a single favourite value into each piece and multiplied, and got whatever number they had hoped for. The trouble is that for most of the seven the honest answer is "somewhere between this and that", and the gap between this and that is often not a factor of two but a factor of a thousand, or a million, or far more. A single value hides that. In 2018 the researchers Anders Sandberg, Eric Drexler and Toby Ord, in a paper called "Dissolving the Fermi Paradox", showed what happens when you keep the uncertainty in: the answer is not a number but a wide spread, and a large part of that spread sits below one civilisation per galaxy. On their reading, the silence is not a paradox at all; it is roughly what you should expect given how little we know.
    </p>
    <p>
      This site follows that method. Each of the seven pieces carries a range, set from the published estimates listed on its page, with a written reason. The multiplication is then run many thousands of times. Each time, a value is picked at random from every piece's range, the seven are multiplied, and the result is kept. The thousands of results are the spread.
    </p>

    <h2>How a value is picked from a range</h2>
    <p>
      For most pieces the range is drawn so that every tenfold step is equally likely: a pick between 0.001 and 1 is as likely to land between 0.001 and 0.01 as between 0.1 and 1. That is the right way to be uncertain about a quantity that could be any of several orders of magnitude, and it is what the 2018 paper did. A piece whose range is narrow enough that tenfold steps make no sense (a share of stars that is known to lie between 0.6 and 1, say) can instead be drawn so that every value is equally likely. One piece, the chance that life starts, has no two ends to draw between, and uses the 2018 paper's own shape: what is picked is the number of times life starts on such a planet over its life, from a bell curve in its powers of ten centred on one start and fifty powers of ten wide, and the chance is one minus e to the minus that number. About half the picks then land near 1 and the rest spread across fifty powers of ten below, which is the published argument in one curve: life is either easy or very hard, and nobody knows which. Each piece's page says which shape it uses.
    </p>

    <h2>The three numbers, and the width</h2>
    <p>
      From the spread come the three numbers on the home page. The first is the middle of the spread: the value of N such that half the results came out above it and half below. The second is the share of results that came out below one, which is the chance that we are the only detectable civilisation in the galaxy. The third is the same for the whole observable universe: the share of results so small that, even multiplied by the number of galaxies there are, they come to fewer than one.
    </p>
    <p>
      Beside them sits the width of the argument. Take the most hopeful published estimate of every piece and multiply, then the most pessimistic and multiply. The two results are the ends of the argument as it has actually been made, and the gap between them is the thing a single number hides.
    </p>
