    <h2>Where the ranges come from</h2>
    <p>
      The ranges are the least certain part of the method, and the rest of the method exists to correct them over time. Each piece's page lists every published estimate on the record, with who made it, when, how, and where to find it. The range is then set by hand from those estimates, with a reason written in plain words. It is not computed from them, because the judgement about which estimates to lean on is the arguable part, and it should be visible and arguable.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every range starts life marked as a first pass. Before it is called settled, it goes through an outside review: the estimates and the reason are put to other readers, including other AI models, with a fixed brief to argue the range up or down, and I rule on every disagreement. Until that has happened for all seven, the home page says so. Each range was first drafted by Claude, the AI I build this project with, from the published estimates, and accepted or moved by me; every change since is a dated revision on the piece's page with the old number, the new one and the reason.
    </p>

    <h2>What moves a number, and what does not</h2>
    <p>
      Fixed, and changed only at a yearly review: the seven pieces, the method of picking from a range, and the rule for setting a range from estimates. Moved whenever the record moves, with a dated line: a piece's range, when a paper or a survey of the kind its page names under "what would move this" is published. Added at any time: a new explanation for the silence, a new paper in the log, a new work on the library shelf. Never changed once published: an explanation's address.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each paper is logged in <a href="/story/">the story so far</a> whether or not it moved anything, and most do not. A paper that says it moved a range must be named by a dated revision on some piece, and the checking script refuses the record otherwise; that cross-check is what keeps the log honest.
    </p>

    <h2>What the explanations are, in this method</h2>
    <p>
      An explanation for the silence is, in the end, a claim about one or more of the seven pieces: that life is rarer than it looks, that civilisations do not last, or that the pieces are fine and we are failing to see what is there. Each explanation's page says which pieces it bears on and in which direction, and what evidence would strengthen or weaken it. The explanations are not given probabilities and are not multiplied into anything. They are the catalogue of arguments the ranges are read against.
    </p>

    <h2>What is not in the method</h2>
    <p>
      The seven pieces are drawn independently, as the 2018 paper's main text does; a world in which life starts easily may also be one in which it becomes intelligent easily, and the method does not yet allow for that. The explanations are not weighed against each other. There is no attempt to update the ranges on the silence itself, which the 2018 paper does in its later sections and which this site may add later with a note here. These are the known gaps, and the yearly review is where they are reconsidered.
    </p>

